    "plainEnglish": "Kaposi's sarcoma is a type of cancer that develops in the cells lining blood vessels and lymph vessels, causing abnormal tissue growth that typically appears as purple, red, or brown patches or nodules on the skin. It's caused by a virus called human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8) and occurs most commonly in people with severely weakened immune systems, such as those with advanced HIV/AIDS or organ transplant recipients. The lesions may start small but can grow and spread to other areas of the skin or internally.",
    "whoUses": "Oncologists, dermatologists, and infectious disease specialists commonly use this code when diagnosing and treating Kaposi's sarcoma, particularly in patients with HIV/AIDS. This code frequently appears on EOB statements for patients receiving chemotherapy, immunotherapy, or other cancer treatments for this condition.",
