    "longDesc": "Kaposi's sarcoma of other sites",
    "plainEnglish": "Kaposi's sarcoma is a cancer that starts in the cells lining blood vessels or lymph vessels, typically appearing as purple, red, or brown patches or lumps on the skin. This code indicates the cancer has developed in parts of the body other than the most common sites (like the skin, oral cavity, or lungs). The disease is caused by a virus called HHV-8 (human herpesvirus 8) and is especially common in people with weakened immune systems, such as those with untreated HIV/AIDS.",
    "whoUses": "Oncologists, infectious disease specialists, and hematologists commonly use this code when diagnosing and treating Kaposi's sarcoma in unusual body locations. It frequently appears on EOB statements for cancer patients, particularly those receiving chemotherapy or immunosuppressive therapy, and is important for tracking treatment and insurance coverage of cancer care.",
