    "shortDesc": "Kaposi's sarcoma, unspecified",
    "longDesc": "Kaposi's sarcoma, unspecified",
    "plainEnglish": "Kaposi's sarcoma is a type of cancer that causes abnormal tissue growth, usually appearing as dark purple, red, or brown patches or bumps on the skin, though it can also affect internal organs like the lungs and digestive system. This cancer is caused by a virus called HHV-8 and most commonly develops in people with severely weakened immune systems, particularly those with untreated HIV/AIDS. The 'unspecified' designation means the doctor has diagnosed Kaposi's sarcoma but hasn't documented which specific part of the body is affected.",
    "whoUses": "Oncologists, infectious disease specialists, and HIV/AIDS specialists use this code when diagnosing and treating Kaposi's sarcoma, particularly in immunocompromised patients. It frequently appears on EOB statements for patients with advanced HIV disease or other severe immune conditions requiring cancer treatment.",
