    "rawCode": "C751",
    "shortDesc": "Malignant neoplasm of pituitary gland",
    "longDesc": "Malignant neoplasm of pituitary gland",
    "plainEnglish": "This code describes cancer that has started in the pituitary gland, a small pea-sized gland at the base of the brain that controls many hormones in your body. When cancer develops here, it means abnormal cells have grown uncontrollably in this gland. Symptoms can include headaches, vision problems, hormonal imbalances (like changes in growth, thyroid function, or cortisol levels), and sometimes nausea or weakness.",
    "whoUses": "Oncologists, neuro-oncologists, neurosurgeons, and endocrinologists use this code when diagnosing and treating pituitary cancer. It commonly appears on EOB statements for patients undergoing imaging studies, biopsies, surgical procedures, chemotherapy, or radiation therapy related to pituitary malignancies.",
