    "plainEnglish": "This code describes cancer that starts in the pineal gland, a tiny pea-sized gland deep in the brain that produces melatonin (a hormone that helps regulate sleep). Pineal gland cancer is very rare and occurs when abnormal cells grow uncontrollably in this gland, potentially affecting sleep cycles and hormone regulation. Symptoms may include headaches, vision problems, balance issues, or sleep disturbances, depending on how large the tumor grows and what structures it presses on.",
    "whoUses": "Oncologists, neuro-oncologists, and neurosurgeons use this code when diagnosing and treating pineal gland cancer. It commonly appears in cancer registries, hospital records, and insurance claims, though it is an uncommon diagnosis that may be referenced on EOB statements when patients undergo tumor-related treatments like surgery, radiation, or chemotherapy.",
