    "plainEnglish": "This code describes cancer that involves multiple endocrine glands (hormone-producing glands) in your body, but the specific glands affected haven't been identified or documented in detail. Rather than being one isolated tumor, this type of cancer affects several glands at once—possibly including the pituitary, thyroid, parathyroid, or adrenal glands—making it a more complex and systemic condition that requires careful coordination of treatment across multiple gland systems.",
    "whoUses": "Oncologists, endocrinologists, and internal medicine specialists use this code when a patient has been diagnosed with cancer involving multiple endocrine glands but the exact combination or primary location needs further clarification. It commonly appears on EOB statements for patients undergoing diagnostic workup or treatment for advanced endocrine cancers, though it's often a temporary coding assignment that may be replaced with more specific codes once testing identifies which glands are involved.",
