    "rawCode": "C9300",
    "shortDesc": "Acute monoblastic/monocytic leukemia, not achieve remission",
    "longDesc": "Acute monoblastic/monocytic leukemia, not having achieved remission",
    "plainEnglish": "Acute monoblastic/monocytic leukemia is a serious blood cancer where immature monocyte cells (a type of white blood cell) multiply rapidly in your bone marrow and bloodstream, crowding out healthy blood cells. The \"not having achieved remission\" part means the cancer is active and has not responded to treatment or the person has not yet gone into remission (a period where cancer cells are no longer detectable). Patients typically experience symptoms like fatigue, frequent infections, bleeding or bruising easily, and swollen lymph nodes or organs.",
    "whoUses": "Hematologists and oncologists use this code when diagnosing and monitoring acute leukemia patients, typically in hospital or specialized cancer center settings. This code appears on EOB statements when patients receive chemotherapy, diagnostic testing, or hospitalization for acute leukemia treatment, and it's commonly used for insurance authorization and billing purposes.",
